str_to_long() ignored strtol() ERANGE overflow, storing the clamped
LONG_MAX/MIN and reporting success.  On ILP32 (i586) str_to_int() then
wrongly succeeds for out-of-range input such as "9999999999", failing
the unit test that asserts refusal (on LP64 the value fits in long and
the int-narrowing check catches it, so only 32-bit builds break).
Check errno after strtol() and fail on ERANGE.

Index: ddcutil-3.0.2/src/util/string_util.c
===================================================================
--- ddcutil-3.0.2.orig/src/util/string_util.c
+++ ddcutil-3.0.2/src/util/string_util.c
@@ -1119,10 +1119,11 @@
             printf("(%s) work = %s\n", __func__, work);
 
          if (has_digits) {
+            errno = 0;
             long result = strtol(work, &endptr, base); // allow hex
             // printf("(%s) sval=%p, endptr=%p, *endptr=|%c| (0x%02x), result=%ld\n",
             //        __func__, sval, endptr, *endptr, *endptr, result);
-            if (*endptr == '\0') {
+            if (*endptr == '\0' && errno != ERANGE) {
                *p_ival = result;
                ok = true;
             }
